{"name":"Plague Brown","hex":"#dfb900","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"plague","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The bubonic plague, the pestilent disease caused by the virulent bacterium Yersinia pestis.","An epidemic or pandemic caused by any pestilence, but especially that caused by the above disease."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To harass, pester or annoy someone persistently or incessantly.","To afflict with a disease or other calamity."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/plague"},{"term":"brown","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A colour like that of chocolate or coffee.","One of the colour balls used in snooker, with a value of 4 points."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having a brown color/colour.","Gloomy."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/brown"}],"entries":[{"title":"Plague (disease)","blocks":[{"text":"Plague is an infectious disease caused by the bacterium Yersinia pestis. Symptoms include fever, weakness, headache and black lips. Usually, this begins one to seven days after exposure. There are three forms of plague, each affecting a different part of the body and causing associated symptoms. Pneumonic plague infects the lungs, causing shortness of breath, coughing and chest pain; bubonic plague affects the lymph nodes, making them swell; and septicemic plague infects the blood and can cause necrosis of tissues."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/15/Yersinia_pestis_fluorescent.jpeg/330px-Yersinia_pestis_fluorescent.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Plague_(disease)","wikidata":"Q133780"},{"title":"Brown","blocks":[{"text":"Brown is a color. It can be thought as a darker, typically desaturated shade of orange, and can often be produced by combining red and yellow with another color, namely blue."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ca/Espresso-roasted_coffee_beans.jpg/330px-Espresso-roasted_coffee_beans.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brown","wikidata":"Q47071"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2f/Plague_-buboes.jpg/500px-Plague_-buboes.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2f/Plague_-buboes.jpg/1280px-Plague_-buboes.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2f/Plague_-buboes.jpg/500px-Plague_-buboes.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Swollen inguinal lymph glands on a person infected with the bubonic plague. The swollen lymph glands are termed buboes from the Greek word for groin, swollen gland: bubo.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APlague_-buboes.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a9/PlagueTypes.jpg/500px-PlagueTypes.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a9/PlagueTypes.jpg/500px-PlagueTypes.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Septicemic plague resulting in necrosis","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APlagueTypes.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/25/Xenopsylla_chepsis_%28oriental_rat_flea%29.jpg/500px-Xenopsylla_chepsis_%28oriental_rat_flea%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/25/Xenopsylla_chepsis_%28oriental_rat_flea%29.jpg/500px-Xenopsylla_chepsis_%28oriental_rat_flea%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Oriental rat flea (Xenopsylla cheopis) engorged with blood after a blood meal. This species of flea is the primary vector for the transmission of Yersinia pestis, the organism responsible for bubonic plague in most plague epidemics in Asia, Africa, and South America. Both male and female fleas feed on blood and can transmit the infection.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AXenopsylla_chepsis_(oriental_rat_flea).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bf/Ulceration_of_flea_bite_cause_by_yersinia_pestis.jpg/500px-Ulceration_of_flea_bite_cause_by_yersinia_pestis.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bf/Ulceration_of_flea_bite_cause_by_yersinia_pestis.jpg/500px-Ulceration_of_flea_bite_cause_by_yersinia_pestis.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A child bitten by a flea infected with the bacterium Yersinia pestis. Y. pestis, a member of the family Yersiniaceae, has caused the bite to become ulcerated.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AUlceration_of_flea_bite_cause_by_yersinia_pestis.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/World_distribution_of_plague_1998.PNG/500px-World_distribution_of_plague_1998.PNG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/World_distribution_of_plague_1998.PNG/500px-World_distribution_of_plague_1998.PNG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Distribution of plague-infected animals 1998","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWorld_distribution_of_plague_1998.PNG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/82/Medved_mzoo.jpg/330px-Medved_mzoo.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/82/Medved_mzoo.jp